s, which pose a challenge for some sparse convolution methods that are only applicable to large filters.'], 'source_chunk_index': [2, 2]}, 'Filter Sizes': {'type': ['task'], 'description': ['The dimensions of the filters used in convolutional layers. Some sparse convolution methods are limited in their applicability to specific filter sizes, such as large filters (e.g., 9x9), which restricts their use in modern CNNs that often employ smaller filters (e.g., 3x3).'], 'source_chunk_index': [2]}, 'AlexNet': {'type': ['method', 'method', 'dataset'], 'description': ['A Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) architecture used as a benchmark in the text, with specific performance metrics provided for its sparse implementation.', 'A specific Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) architecture used as a benchmark in the document for evaluating the performance of sparse CNN implementations.', 'A convolutional neural network architecture used to evaluate the performance of Holistic SparseCNN.'], 'source_chunk_index': [3, 3, 7]}, 'Speedups of convolution layers (3.4x and 7.3x)': {'type': ['method'], 'description': ['Performance metrics achieved by the optimized sparse CNN implementation, indicating the speed increase over the best dense direct convolution performance on Xeon and Atom processors, respectively.'], 'source_chunk_index': [3]}, 'AlexNet classification throughput (2371 and 120 images per second)': {'type': ['method'], 'description': ['Performance metrics representing the classification speed of AlexNet, measured in images per second, on respective platforms.'], 'source_chunk_index': [3]}, 'Xeon': {'type': ['method'], 'description': ['A processor on which the performance of the sparse CNN implementation is evaluated and compared against dense convolution.'], 'source_chunk_index': [3]}, 'Atom': {'type': ['method'], 'description': ['Another processor on which the performance of the sparse CNN implementation is evaluated and compared against dense convolution.'], 'source_chunk_in